[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 4. libc / stdlib
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Everything comes from `crate::jsi` (`use crate::jsi::*;`): `malloc`, `realloc`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `free`, `memcpy`, `memmove`, `memset`, `memcmp`, `strlen`, `strcmp`, `strncmp`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strcpy`, `strchr`, `strrchr`, `strstr`, `qsort`, `snprintf`, `sprintf`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printf`, `fprintf`, `putchar`, `fputs`, `fputc`, `strtod`, `strtol`, `time`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `mktime`, `localtime`, `gmtime`, `floor`, `ceil`, `fmod`, `pow`, `sqrt`, `fabs`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `asin`, `acos`, `atan`, `atan2`, `exp`, `log`, `abort`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `stdout`, `stderr`, plus `isnan(x)`, `isinf(x)`, `isfinite(x)`, `signbit(x)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (these four take `f64` and return `bool`), `INT_MAX`, `INT_MIN`, `INFINITY`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `NAN`, `M_PI`, ... .
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Always use these libc functions** for formatting and math — never Rust's
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `format!`/`f64::sqrt` — so the output bytes are identical.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C string literals: `c"hello".as_ptr()`. `strlen(s)` returns `size_t`; cast when
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the C code assigns it to an `int`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printf`/`snprintf` are variadic; calling them is fine on stable Rust.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Remember C promotion rules: pass `c_int` for `%d`/`%c`, `f64` for `%g`/`%f`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `*const c_char` for `%s`, `*const c_void`/pointer for `%p`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 5. Characters and arithmetic
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `c_char` is signed. Read a character as `let c = *p as c_int;` and compare
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   with `'a' as c_int` / `0`. Write with `... as c_char`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Overflow checks are disabled in the release profile, so `a + b` on `c_int`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   wraps just like C. Prefer `wrapping_add`/`wrapping_mul` when the C code
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   clearly relies on wrapping.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **double -> int casts**: Rust `as` saturates, x86 C truncates to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `INT_MIN` on overflow. Where the C code does an implicit/explicit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `(int)someDouble` and the value may be out of range, use
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `crate::jsi::d2i(x)` (and `d2u(x)` for `unsigned`). For in-range values
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `x as c_int` is fine.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Integer division/`%` semantics are the same (truncating).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `x % y` on `f64`: use `fmod(x, y)`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 6. Errors and exceptions
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Macros defined in `lib.rs`, usable unqualified inside any module:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m js_error!(J, c"stack underflow!".as_ptr());
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m js_typeerror!(J, c"'%s' is read-only".as_ptr(), name);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m js_rangeerror!(J, c"invalid string length".as_ptr());
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m js_referenceerror!(J, c"'%s' is not defined".as_ptr(), str);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m js_syntaxerror!(J, c"unexpected token: %s".as_ptr(), jsY_tokenstring(t));
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m js_urierror!(J, c"invalid escape sequence".as_ptr());
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m js_evalerror!(J, c"...".as_ptr());
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m They format into a `[c_char; 256]` with `snprintf` exactly like the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `vsnprintf(buf, sizeof buf, fmt, ap)`, then throw. They diverge (`!`), so no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `return` is needed after them — but note Rust needs the macro invocation to be
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in statement/expression position; when the C code relies on the call diverging
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m in a value context write `js_typeerror!(...)` as the tail expression.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `try`/`catch`. The C idiom
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m if (js_try(J)) { HANDLER }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m BODY
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m js_endtry(J);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m becomes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m if crate::except::js_try_run(J, || { BODY; js_endtry(J); }) { HANDLER }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    188[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (the closure body must end with `js_endtry(J)`, mirroring the C). To get values
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    189[0m:[38;5;10m[49m out of the closure declare them before it and assign inside:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    190[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    191[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    192[0m:[38;5;10m[49m let mut s: *const c_char = core::ptr::null();
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    193[0m:[38;5;10m[49m if crate::except::js_try_run(J, || { s = js_tostring(J, idx); js_endtry(J); }) {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    194[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     js_pop(J, 1);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    195[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     return error;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    196[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    197[0m:[38;5;10m[49m return s;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    198[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    199[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    200[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## 7. Cross-module calls
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    201[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
